<article class="env-lemma" id="0G2D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G2D">Lemma <span data-tag="0G2D">68.9.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $j : V \to Y$ be a quasi-compact open immersion of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $\pi : Z \to V$ be an integral morphism. Then there exists an integral morphism $\nu : Y' \to Y$ such that $Z$ is $V$-isomorphic to the inverse image of $V$ in $Y'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since both $j$ and $\pi $ are quasi-compact and separated, so is $j \circ \pi $. Let $\nu : Y' \to Y$ be the normalization of $Y$ in $Z$, see Morphisms of Spaces, Section <a href="/tag/0BAZ" data-tag="0BAZ">67.48</a>. Of course $\nu $ is integral, see Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0823" data-tag="0823">67.48.5</a>. The final statement follows formally from Morphisms of Spaces, Lemmas <a href="/tag/0ABP" data-tag="0ABP">67.48.4</a> and <a href="/tag/0825" data-tag="0825">67.48.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>